[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Streets of Jeonju Hanok Village. Although the first impression is positive, complaints remain about the limited variety of food and activities. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025



Meanwhile, a 2022 policy report by the Korea Culture and Tourism Institute titled &ldquo;Research on Strategies to Expand the Base of the Traditional Cultural Industry&rdquo; stated that although hanbok, hansik (Korean food), and hanji (traditional paper) are representative cultural assets, they still remain primarily experience-based services focused on foreign consumers. The report highlighted a lack of regional brand identity, integration with modern design, and the development of integrated stay-type content.

It further pointed out that traditional cultural content is mostly centered on heritage experiences or local government-led festivals and has not grown into a private sector-driven content industry. In order to enhance the sustainability of traditional culture, the report proposed moving beyond short-term public support to long-term, region-based branding strategies, creating combined experience-tourism products linking hanbok, hansik, and hanji, modernizing menus using local ingredients and reflecting regional identity in branding, strengthening stay-type content to increase repeat visitation, and building collaborative ecosystems involving local residents and private companies.Although tourism based on traditional culture remains popular, especially around Jeonju Hanok Village, the sustainability of experience content and private-sector involvement remains an unresolved issue. For Jeonbuk&rsquo;s strategy to be effective, it must go beyond top-down administration to develop experiences that meet tourist expectations and create partnerships locals can genuinely feel. The province&rsquo;s next challenge lies in how it designs this sustainable ecosystem

